        "**Calcium imaging** reports changes in intracellular calcium through changing fluorescence of a calcium-sensitive dye. Calcium rises in a neuron when it is excited, so the fluorescence signal tracks activity. Because many cells can be imaged at the same time, the method complements measurements made from one neuron at a time and shows how a population responds to the same stimulus.",
